#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I
"""pr-wait: ONE waiter per PR head for CI and Codex's review.

  pr-wait PR [--repo OWNER/NAME] [--head SHA] [--since ISO] [--out PREFIX] [--timeout S]
             [--interval S] [--codex-stuck S] [--ci-grace S] [--no-codex] [--json]

Why (2026-10-23): three waiters per head (CI, Codex, adversary) were two thirds of the
notification turns of a 579-turn lead transcript, and the load watcher found eight duplicate
waiters on 2026-10-20. This is the one aggregating waiter: it wakes the lead once, when
  1. every CI check on the exact head SHA is finished (`check-runs` plus commit statuses of
     that SHA through `gh api`, not the PR's current checks), and
  2. Codex's review of that exact head is terminal (AGENTS.md code review rule 9): the comment
     carrying `<!-- codex-pull-request-review-summary -->` holds `<!-- codex-review-state:v1
     {...} -->`, edited in place; `code_review.commit_sha` must equal the head and
     `code_review.status` be `completed` or `failed` (both terminal; a marker for another sha
     never matches).
Then it prints ONE line and writes, each atomically (temp file beside it, then os.replace):
  PREFIX-checks.json   the normalized checks (name, kind, state, conclusion) of the head
  PREFIX-codex.json    the state marker of the head (the newest marker seen when none matches; {} none)
  PREFIX-findings.json Codex's inline findings for the head
                       (`null` when not collected: --no-codex, or the wait ended before Codex did)
  PREFIX-open-findings.json  every unresolved Codex review thread of any head (see Open findings)
  PREFIX-summary.json  the one-line result as JSON
PREFIX defaults to ~/.local/state/pr-wait/<owner>__<name>-pr<N>-<head12>. Output files are written on
every end except exit 2 and 4 (nothing was waited for).
Both conditions are re-judged on every poll (a check suite registered late, a re-run, a re-requested
review); it finishes only when both hold on the same poll.

Only the Codex app is believed: a state marker counts only from a comment whose author is
`chatgpt-codex-connector[bot]` (type Bot) and whose `performed_via_github_app.slug` is
`chatgpt-codex-connector`; the repo is public, so anyone can post a comment holding the markers.

Findings for the head are the PR's inline comments (`pulls/N/comments`) that carry
`<!-- codex-code-review-finding:v1 -->`, are authored by that bot, and belong to the head: their
`original_commit_id` is the head, or their `pull_request_review_id` is a `pulls/N/reviews` entry whose
`commit_id` is the head. No time window (2026-10-23, adversary round 1: Codex's own receipt comments
quote `@codex review`, so a "newest request" moved past real findings; PR 12 on 2026-10-20). GitHub
re-anchors `commit_id` of a finding on an unchanged line, but not these two. `--since ISO` is an optional
extra lower bound on created_at.

Open findings: a finding of an EARLIER head stays open until its review thread is resolved (PR 32,
2026-10-23: Codex left a P1 on head 3c40470, two pushes followed, the marker for the new head said
`has_unresolved_findings: true` and the head-only filter said 0). So the waiter also reads the PR's review
threads (`gh api graphql`, `reviewThreads.isResolved`, the authority) and lists every unresolved Codex
thread of any head, outdated or not, in PREFIX-open-findings.json (`null` when not collected). Fixing a
finding means fixing the code, then resolving its thread.
Summary fields: has_any_findings and has_unresolved_findings (from the marker), findings_for_head,
open_findings (unresolved Codex threads, outdated or not) and open_findings_outdated (the outdated
subset), findings_mismatch (list of reasons; the marker and the threads disagree: has_unresolved_findings
true with no open thread, false with some, or has_any_findings true with no Codex finding comment at
all; never print a clean 0 over one), merge_ready and merge_blockers. merge_ready is true only when: at
least one CI check ran and all passed, Codex completed on this head, zero open Codex threads, no
mismatch, and the marker states has_unresolved_findings as a boolean (otherwise `marker-unverified`).
The exit code does not carry it: the caller reads merge_ready.

A `gh` failure (or empty `gh` output) is "unknown", never "done": it is retried on the next poll and
reported in the summary (`errors`) when the wait ends. A PR that does not exist (HTTP 404) is a usage error.

Codex stuck: no terminal marker for the head `--codex-stuck` seconds (default 2700) after the wait
started (marker absent) or after the marker was first seen non-terminal (running) ends the wait with
result `codex-stuck`, never a silent timeout. CI with no checks at all counts as pending for
`--ci-grace` seconds (default 120) after the wait started, because a fresh push registers its check runs
a little later; after that, zero checks is "no checks" and finished.

Exit: 0 done (CI finished and Codex terminal, whatever each said: read the files and `merge_ready`),
      1 timed out, 2 bad usage, no such PR, or the lock takeover guard held by a live pid for 30 s,
      3 Codex stuck, 4 another pr-wait for the same
      repo + PR + head is alive (its pid and output prefix on stderr: never re-arm it),
      5 the PR head moved (a new push; the new head is in the line and the summary),
      6 timed out while `gh` was failing (the last poll did not get through).
Duplicate guard: a lock file `lock-<repo>-<pr>-<head>` in $PR_WAIT_STATE_DIR (default
~/.local/state/pr-wait), created atomically with its content (temp file, then os.link), holding the pid
and a process start identity (Linux: /proc/<pid>/stat starttime, immune to wall-clock steps; macOS:
`ps -o lstart=`); a dead pid, or a live pid whose identity differs (pid reuse), is stale and taken over
under a takeover guard that holds its taker's pid and start and is broken by liveness.
"""

import argparse
import atexit
import json
import os
import re
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from datetime import datetime, timezone
from pathlib import Path

DEFAULT_REPO = "djbclark/djbclark-ade"
SUMMARY_MARK = "<!-- codex-pull-request-review-summary -->"
STATE_RE = re.compile(r"codex-review-state:v1\s+")
FINDING_MARK = "codex-code-review-finding:v1"
CODEX_LOGIN = "chatgpt-codex-connector[bot]"
CODEX_APP = "chatgpt-codex-connector"
CODEX_GQL_LOGIN = "chatgpt-codex-connector"  # GraphQL author login: no [bot] suffix
TERMINAL = ("completed", "failed")
PASS_CONCLUSIONS = ("success", "neutral", "skipped")
GH_TIMEOUT = 120

EXIT_DONE, EXIT_TIMEOUT, EXIT_USAGE, EXIT_STUCK, EXIT_DUP, EXIT_MOVED, EXIT_GH = 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6


class GhError(Exception):
    def __init__(self, msg: str, not_found: bool = False) -> None:
        super().__init__(msg)
        self.not_found = not_found


# ---- gh -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def gh(*args: str) -> str:
    """Run `gh` with an argv list; any failure is a GhError, never an empty success."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(
            ["gh", *args], check=False, capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=GH_TIMEOUT
        )
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
        raise GhError(f"gh {args[0]} {args[1] if len(args) > 1 else ''}: {e}") from e
    if p.returncode != 0:
        err = (p.stderr or p.stdout).strip().replace("\n", " ")[:300]
        raise GhError(f"gh {' '.join(args[:2])}: rc={p.returncode} {err}", "HTTP 404" in err)
    return p.stdout


def parse_pages(text: str) -> list:
    """`gh api --paginate` prints one JSON document PER PAGE, concatenated: decode them all."""
    dec = json.JSONDecoder()
    docs, i, n = [], 0, len(text)
    while True:
        while i < n and text[i].isspace():
            i += 1
        if i >= n:
            return docs
        try:
            doc, i = dec.raw_decode(text, i)
        except ValueError as e:
            raise GhError(f"unparsable gh output at byte {i}: {e}") from e
        docs.append(doc)


def api_items(endpoint: str, key: str | None = None) -> list:
    """All items of a paginated list endpoint; `key` names the array inside an object page."""
    items: list = []
    docs = parse_pages(gh("api", "--paginate", endpoint))
    if not docs:
        raise GhError(f"{endpoint}: empty gh output")
    for doc in docs:
        if key is not None:
            part = doc.get(key) if isinstance(doc, dict) else None
        else:
            part = doc
        if isinstance(part, list):
            items.extend(part)
        else:
            raise GhError(f"{endpoint}: unexpected page shape {type(doc).__name__}")
    return items


def pr_head(repo: str, pr: int) -> str:
    try:
        doc = json.loads(gh("api", f"repos/{repo}/pulls/{pr}"))
        sha = doc["head"]["sha"]
    except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) as e:
        raise GhError(f"pulls/{pr}: unexpected shape: {e}") from e
    if not isinstance(sha, str) or not sha:
        raise GhError(f"pulls/{pr}: no head sha")
    return sha


# ---- CI -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def fetch_checks(repo: str, sha: str) -> list[dict]:
    runs = api_items(f"repos/{repo}/commits/{sha}/check-runs?per_page=100", "check_runs")
    statuses = api_items(f"repos/{repo}/commits/{sha}/status?per_page=100", "statuses")
    out = []
    for r in runs:
        done = r.get("status") == "completed"
        out.append({"name": r.get("name"), "kind": "check-run", "state": "finished" if done else "pending",
                    "conclusion": r.get("conclusion") if done else None})
    for s in statuses:
        st = s.get("state")
        out.append({"name": s.get("context"), "kind": "status",
                    "state": "pending" if st == "pending" else "finished",
                    "conclusion": None if st == "pending" else st})
    return out


def ci_counts(checks: list[dict]) -> dict:
    pending = sum(1 for c in checks if c["state"] == "pending")
    passed = sum(1 for c in checks if c["state"] == "finished" and c["conclusion"] in PASS_CONCLUSIONS)
    return {"total": len(checks), "pass": passed, "fail": len(checks) - pending - passed, "pending": pending}


# ---- Codex ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def parse_state(body: str) -> dict | None:
    m = STATE_RE.search(body)
    if not m:
        return None
    try:
        doc, _ = json.JSONDecoder().raw_decode(body, m.end())
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return doc if isinstance(doc, dict) else None


def is_codex_user(c: dict) -> bool:
    """Authored by the Codex app's bot account; anyone can post a comment with Codex's markers."""
    u = c.get("user")
    return isinstance(u, dict) and u.get("login") == CODEX_LOGIN and u.get("type") == "Bot"


def is_codex_app_comment(c: dict) -> bool:
    app = c.get("performed_via_github_app")
    return is_codex_user(c) and isinstance(app, dict) and app.get("slug") == CODEX_APP


def codex_state_for(comments: list[dict], head: str) -> tuple[dict | None, dict | None]:
    """(marker whose code_review.commit_sha is head, newest marker of any head), Codex app comments only."""
    marked = []
    for c in comments:
        body = c.get("body") or ""
        if SUMMARY_MARK in body and is_codex_app_comment(c):
            st = parse_state(body)
            if st is not None:
                marked.append((c.get("updated_at") or c.get("created_at") or "", st))
    marked.sort(key=lambda t: t[0])
    newest = marked[-1][1] if marked else None
    match = None
    for _, st in marked:
        cr = st.get("code_review")
        if isinstance(cr, dict) and cr.get("commit_sha") == head:
            match = st
    return match, newest


def code_review(state: dict | None) -> dict:
    cr = (state or {}).get("code_review")
    return cr if isinstance(cr, dict) else {}


def parse_ts(s: str) -> datetime:
    d = datetime.fromisoformat(s.strip().replace("Z", "+00:00"))
    return d if d.tzinfo else d.replace(tzinfo=timezone.utc)


THREADS_QUERY = (
    "query($owner:String!,$name:String!,$number:Int!,$cursor:String){repository(owner:$owner,name:$name){"
    "pullRequest(number:$number){reviewThreads(first:100,after:$cursor){pageInfo{hasNextPage endCursor} "
    "nodes{isResolved isOutdated comments(first:1){nodes{databaseId author{login} path originalCommit{oid}}}}}}}}"
)


def fetch_threads(repo: str, pr: int) -> list[dict]:
    """Every review thread of the PR: its first comment's databaseId, author, path, original commit and
    whether the thread is resolved / outdated. GraphQL `reviewThreads.isResolved` is the authority on
    whether a finding is still open (PR 32, 2026-10-23: an earlier head's P1 stayed open past two pushes).
    Owner and name go through `-f` (strings: `-F` would turn a digits-only name into an integer)."""
    owner, name = repo.split("/", 1)
    threads: list[dict] = []
    cursor: str | None = None
    seen: set[str] = set()
    while True:
        args = ["api", "graphql", "-f", f"owner={owner}", "-f", f"name={name}", "-F", f"number={pr}",
                "-f", f"query={THREADS_QUERY}"]
        if cursor is not None:
            args += ["-f", f"cursor={cursor}"]
        try:
            doc = json.loads(gh(*args))
            rt = doc["data"]["repository"]["pullRequest"]["reviewThreads"]
            nodes, info = rt["nodes"], rt["pageInfo"]
        except (ValueError, KeyError, TypeError) as e:
            raise GhError(f"graphql reviewThreads: unusable answer: {e}") from e
        if doc.get("errors"):
            raise GhError(f"graphql reviewThreads: errors: {str(doc['errors'])[:200]}")
        for n in nodes:
            first = ((n.get("comments") or {}).get("nodes") or [None])[0]
            if not isinstance(first, dict):
                continue
            threads.append({"databaseId": first.get("databaseId"),
                            "author": (first.get("author") or {}).get("login"),
                            "path": first.get("path"),
                            "original_commit_id": (first.get("originalCommit") or {}).get("oid"),
                            "isResolved": bool(n.get("isResolved")), "isOutdated": bool(n.get("isOutdated"))})
        if not info.get("hasNextPage"):
            return threads
        cursor = info.get("endCursor")
        if not cursor or cursor in seen:
            raise GhError("graphql reviewThreads: hasNextPage without a new endCursor")
        seen.add(cursor)


def fetch_review_state(repo: str, pr: int, head: str, since: str | None) -> dict:
    """Codex's findings: `for_head` (selected by head and author, never by a time window) and `open`.

    A comment belongs to the head when its `original_commit_id` is the head or its review
    (`pull_request_review_id`) is a `pulls/N/reviews` entry whose `commit_id` is the head: GitHub
    re-anchors `commit_id` of a finding on an unchanged line, but not these two (PR 12, 2026-10-20).
    A later Codex receipt or a quoted `@codex review` therefore cannot hide a finding.

    `open` is every unresolved Codex review thread of ANY head (outdated or not): a thread whose first
    comment is a Codex finding (REST: Codex bot, finding mark) and whose GraphQL author is the Codex app.
    `finding_comments` counts all Codex finding comments, for the marker cross-check."""
    t0 = parse_ts(since) if since else None
    reviews = api_items(f"repos/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/reviews?per_page=100")
    head_reviews = {r.get("id") for r in reviews if r.get("commit_id") == head}
    findings = [c for c in api_items(f"repos/{repo}/pulls/{pr}/comments?per_page=100")
                if FINDING_MARK in (c.get("body") or "") and is_codex_user(c)]
    for_head = []
    for c in findings:
        if c.get("original_commit_id") != head and c.get("pull_request_review_id") not in head_reviews:
            continue
        if t0 is not None and not (c.get("created_at") and parse_ts(c["created_at"]) >= t0):
            continue
        for_head.append({"id": c.get("id"), "path": c.get("path"), "line": c.get("line") or c.get("original_line"),
                         "created_at": c.get("created_at"), "original_commit_id": c.get("original_commit_id"),
                         "body": (c.get("body") or "")[:600]})
    by_id = {c.get("id"): c for c in findings}
    open_threads = []
    for t in fetch_threads(repo, pr):
        c = by_id.get(t["databaseId"])
        if t["author"] != CODEX_GQL_LOGIN or c is None or t["isResolved"]:
            continue
        body = (c.get("body") or "").strip()
        open_threads.append({"id": t["databaseId"], "path": t["path"] or c.get("path"),
                             "line": c.get("line") or c.get("original_line"),
                             "original_commit_id": t["original_commit_id"] or c.get("original_commit_id"),
                             "isOutdated": t["isOutdated"],
                             "title": (body.splitlines() or [""])[0][:200]})
    return {"for_head": for_head, "open": open_threads, "finding_comments": len(findings)}


def assess(no_codex: bool, ci_state: str, counts: dict, codex_status: str, cr: dict, review: dict | None) -> dict:
    """The findings and merge_ready fields of the summary.

    Mismatch (the marker and the threads disagree, so never a clean "0"): the marker says
    `has_unresolved_findings: true` and no open Codex thread was found, or `false` and some was; or
    `has_any_findings: true` and no Codex finding comment exists at all. merge_ready needs: every CI check
    passed (at least one), Codex completed on this head, zero open Codex threads (outdated or not), no
    mismatch, and a marker that states has_unresolved_findings as a boolean."""
    has_any = cr.get("has_any_findings")
    has_unres = cr.get("has_unresolved_findings")
    mismatch: list[str] = []
    blockers: list[str] = []
    for_head = open_n = outdated_n = None
    if review is not None:
        for_head, open_n = len(review["for_head"]), len(review["open"])
        outdated_n = sum(1 for t in review["open"] if t["isOutdated"])
        if has_unres is True and open_n == 0:
            mismatch.append("marker has_unresolved_findings=true but no open Codex thread found")
        if has_unres is False and open_n > 0:
            mismatch.append(f"marker has_unresolved_findings=false but {open_n} open Codex thread(s) found")
        if has_any is True and review["finding_comments"] == 0:
            mismatch.append("marker has_any_findings=true but no Codex finding comment found")
    if no_codex:
        blockers.append("codex-skipped")
    else:
        if codex_status != "completed":
            blockers.append(f"codex-{codex_status}")
        if review is None:
            blockers.append("findings-not-collected")
        elif not isinstance(has_unres, bool):
            blockers.append("marker-unverified")
    if ci_state == "no-checks":
        blockers.append("no-checks")
    elif ci_state != "done":
        blockers.append("ci-pending")
    if counts["fail"]:
        blockers.append("ci-failed")
    if open_n:
        blockers.append("open-findings")
    if mismatch:
        blockers.append("findings-mismatch")
    return {"has_any_findings": has_any, "has_unresolved_findings": has_unres, "findings_for_head": for_head,
            "open_findings": open_n, "open_findings_outdated": outdated_n, "findings_mismatch": mismatch,
            "merge_ready": not blockers, "merge_blockers": blockers}


# ---- files ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def write_atomic(path: str, doc: object) -> None:
    tmp = f"{path}.tmp.{os.getpid()}"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(doc, f, indent=1)
        f.write("\n")
    os.replace(tmp, path)


# ---- duplicate guard ------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def parse_proc_stat(raw: str) -> str | None:
    """starttime (field 22, jiffies since boot) of a /proc/<pid>/stat line; the comm field may hold ')'."""
    try:
        return "proc:" + raw.rsplit(")", 1)[1].split()[19]
    except IndexError:
        return None


def proc_start(pid: int) -> str | None:
    """An identity for the process that survives pid reuse; None when the pid is gone.

    Linux: /proc/<pid>/stat starttime (jiffies since boot, so a wall-clock step cannot change it).
    Elsewhere (macOS has no /proc): `ps -o lstart=`, which is stored absolutely there."""
    try:
        return parse_proc_stat(Path(f"/proc/{pid}/stat").read_text())
    except FileNotFoundError:
        if Path("/proc/self/stat").exists():
            return None  # Linux and the pid is gone
    except OSError:
        pass
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(["ps", "-o", "lstart=", "-p", str(pid)], check=False, capture_output=True,
                           text=True, timeout=10, env={"LC_ALL": "C", "PATH": os.environ.get("PATH", "")})
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    out = " ".join(p.stdout.split())
    return out if p.returncode == 0 and out else None


def lock_live(doc: object) -> bool:
    if not isinstance(doc, dict) or not isinstance(doc.get("pid"), int) or not doc.get("start"):
        return False
    return proc_start(doc["pid"]) == doc["start"]


def read_lock(path: Path) -> object:
    try:
        return json.loads(path.read_text())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None


class LockBusy(Exception):
    pass


def create_exclusive(path: Path, doc: dict) -> bool:
    """Create `path` already holding `doc`: write a temp file beside it, then os.link (EEXIST = held).

    O_EXCL then write would leave an empty file a second starter could read as stale."""
    tmp = Path(f"{path}.tmp.{os.getpid()}.{time.monotonic_ns()}")
    tmp.write_text(json.dumps(doc))
    try:
        os.link(tmp, path)
        return True
    except FileExistsError:
        return False
    finally:
        tmp.unlink(missing_ok=True)


def acquire_lock(path: Path, info: dict) -> dict | None:
    """Take the lock. Returns None when taken, else the LIVE holder's lock doc.

    A stale lock is taken over under a guard file (`<lock>.takeover`, holding the taker's pid and start,
    broken by liveness, not age) so two takers cannot unlink each other's fresh lock. Residual window:
    two breakers of one orphaned guard can race; the lock itself is still created atomically."""
    guard = Path(f"{path}.takeover")
    ginfo = {"pid": info["pid"], "start": info["start"]}
    deadline = time.monotonic() + 30
    while True:
        if create_exclusive(path, info):
            return None
        doc = read_lock(path)
        if lock_live(doc):
            return doc  # type: ignore[return-value]
        if not create_exclusive(guard, ginfo):
            if not lock_live(read_lock(guard)):
                guard.unlink(missing_ok=True)  # its taker died inside the guard
                continue
            if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                raise LockBusy(f"the lock takeover guard {guard} is held by a live pid for 30 s")
            time.sleep(0.05)
            continue
        try:
            drop_if_stale(path)
        finally:
            gdoc = read_lock(guard)
            if isinstance(gdoc, dict) and gdoc.get("pid") == info["pid"]:
                guard.unlink(missing_ok=True)


def drop_if_stale(path: Path) -> None:
    """Unlink the lock only if a file is there and is not a live waiter's.

    Called inside the takeover guard. A lock that is MISSING must stay missing: another taker may have
    just created a fresh one in the window between our stale read and this reread (adversary round 2, L1);
    an existing but unparseable lock is stale (the writer never leaves one)."""
    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return
    try:
        doc = json.loads(text)
    except ValueError:
        doc = None
    if not lock_live(doc):
        path.unlink(missing_ok=True)


def release_lock(path: Path, pid: int) -> None:
    doc = read_lock(path)
    if isinstance(doc, dict) and doc.get("pid") == pid:
        path.unlink(missing_ok=True)


# ---- the wait -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------


def number(s: str) -> float:
    try:
        v = float(s)
    except ValueError:
        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(f"needs a number of seconds, got '{s}'") from None
    if v < 0:
        raise argparse.ArgumentTypeError(f"must not be negative, got '{s}'")
    return v


class Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
    def error(self, message: str) -> None:  # argparse exits 2 already; keep the usage text short
        self.exit(EXIT_USAGE, f"pr-wait: {message}\n")


def build_parser() -> Parser:
    p = Parser(prog="pr-wait", description="ONE waiter per PR head: CI checks and Codex's review. "
               "Exit: 0 done, 1 timeout, 2 usage/no such PR, 3 Codex stuck, 4 duplicate waiter alive, "
               "5 head moved, 6 timeout while gh was failing.")
    p.add_argument("pr", type=int, help="pull request number")
    p.add_argument("--repo", default=DEFAULT_REPO, help=f"OWNER/NAME (default {DEFAULT_REPO})")
    p.add_argument("--head", help="head SHA to wait on (default: the PR's current head)")
    p.add_argument("--since", help="optional extra lower bound (ISO time) on a finding's created_at; findings "
                   "are selected by head, not by time")
    p.add_argument("--out", help="output prefix (default ~/.local/state/pr-wait/<repo>-pr<N>-<head12>)")
    p.add_argument("--timeout", type=number, default=3600, help="seconds before giving up (default 3600)")
    p.add_argument("--interval", type=number, default=30, help="seconds between polls (default 30)")
    p.add_argument("--codex-stuck", type=number, default=2700, help="seconds before a non-terminal Codex "
                   "review is reported stuck (default 2700)")
    p.add_argument("--ci-grace", type=number, default=120, help="seconds a head with no checks yet counts as "
                   "pending (default 120)")
    p.add_argument("--no-codex", action="store_true", help="wait for CI only")
    p.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="print the summary as one JSON line")
    return p


def state_dir() -> Path:
    return Path(os.environ.get("PR_WAIT_STATE_DIR") or Path.home() / ".local/state/pr-wait")


def main(argv: list[str]) -> int:
    a = build_parser().parse_args(argv)
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[\w.-]+/[\w.-]+", a.repo):
        return usage(f"--repo must be OWNER/NAME, got '{a.repo}'")
    if a.head is not None and not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-fA-F]{40}|[0-9a-fA-F]{64}", a.head):
        return usage(f"--head must be a full commit SHA (40 hex), got '{a.head}'")
    if a.since is not None:
        try:
            parse_ts(a.since)
        except ValueError:
            return usage(f"--since must be an ISO time, got '{a.since}'")
    if a.pr < 1:
        return usage("PR must be a positive number")

    start = time.monotonic()
    deadline = start + a.timeout
    errors: list[str] = []

    # resolve the head; a gh failure is retried, a missing PR is a usage error
    head = a.head
    if head is None:
        while True:
            try:
                head = pr_head(a.repo, a.pr)
                break
            except GhError as e:
                if e.not_found:
                    return usage(f"no such PR: {a.repo}#{a.pr} ({e})")
                errors = [str(e)]
                if time.monotonic() >= deadline:
                    print(f"PR {a.pr}: timeout, gh failed all along: {e}")
                    return EXIT_GH
                time.sleep(min(a.interval, max(0.0, deadline - time.monotonic())))
    head = head.lower()

    sd = state_dir()
    sd.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
    slug = a.repo.replace("/", "__")
    out = a.out or str(sd / f"{slug}-pr{a.pr}-{head[:12]}")
    lock = sd / f"lock-{slug}-{a.pr}-{head}"
    me = os.getpid()
    my_start = proc_start(me)
    if my_start is None:
        return usage("cannot read this process's start time (/proc/<pid>/stat on Linux, `ps -o lstart=` elsewhere)")
    try:
        held = acquire_lock(lock, {"pid": me, "start": my_start, "repo": a.repo, "pr": a.pr,
                                   "head": head, "out": out})
    except LockBusy as e:
        return usage(str(e))
    if held is not None:
        print(f"pr-wait: already waiting on {a.repo}#{a.pr} at {head[:12]}: pid {held.get('pid')}, "
              f"output prefix {held.get('out')} (read its files; never re-arm a live waiter)", file=sys.stderr)
        return EXIT_DUP
    atexit.register(release_lock, lock, me)
    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, lambda *_: sys.exit(143))
    Path(out).parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)

    s = {"checks": None, "codex_match": None, "codex_newest": None, "review": None,
         "ci_done": False, "codex_done": a.no_codex, "since": None, "since_source": None,
         "codex_clock": start, "running_seen": False}

    def finish(result: str, code: int, new_head: str | None = None) -> int:
        checks = s["checks"] or []
        counts = ci_counts(checks)
        cr = code_review(s["codex_match"])
        newest_cr = code_review(s["codex_newest"])
        if a.no_codex:
            codex = {"status": "skipped"}
        else:
            codex = {"status": cr.get("status") or ("absent" if not newest_cr else "other-head"),
                     "commit_sha": cr.get("commit_sha"), "newest_seen_commit_sha": newest_cr.get("commit_sha")}
        ci_state = "done" if s["ci_done"] else "pending"
        if s["ci_done"] and not checks:
            ci_state = "no-checks"
        judged = assess(a.no_codex, ci_state, counts, codex["status"], cr, s["review"])
        summary = {"result": result, "exit": code, "repo": a.repo, "pr": a.pr, "head": head,
                   "new_head": new_head, "ci": {"state": ci_state, **counts}, "codex": codex,
                   **judged, "since": s["since"], "since_source": s["since_source"], "errors": errors[-3:],
                   "elapsed_s": round(time.monotonic() - start, 1), "out": out}
        write_atomic(f"{out}-checks.json", checks)
        write_atomic(f"{out}-codex.json", None if a.no_codex else (s["codex_match"] or s["codex_newest"] or {}))
        write_atomic(f"{out}-findings.json", None if s["review"] is None else s["review"]["for_head"])
        write_atomic(f"{out}-open-findings.json", None if s["review"] is None else s["review"]["open"])
        write_atomic(f"{out}-summary.json", summary)
        if a.json:
            print(json.dumps(summary, separators=(",", ":")))
        else:
            parts = [f"PR {a.pr} at {head[:12]}: {result}",
                     f"CI {ci_state} ({counts['pass']} pass, {counts['fail']} fail, {counts['pending']} pending)"]
            if not a.no_codex:
                parts.append(f"codex {codex['status']}")
            if s["review"] is None:
                parts.append("findings not collected")
            else:
                parts.append(f"findings for the head: {len(s['review']['for_head'])}"
                             + (f" (since {s['since']})" if s["since"] else "")
                             + f"; open Codex findings: {judged['open_findings']}"
                             f" ({judged['open_findings_outdated']} outdated)")
            if judged["findings_mismatch"]:
                parts.append("FINDINGS-MISMATCH: " + " / ".join(judged["findings_mismatch"]))
            parts.append("merge_ready: " + ("yes" if judged["merge_ready"]
                                            else "no (" + ", ".join(judged["merge_blockers"]) + ")"))
            if new_head:
                parts.append(f"new head {new_head[:12]}")
            if errors and code in (EXIT_TIMEOUT, EXIT_GH):
                parts.append(f"last gh error: {errors[-1]}")
            print("; ".join(parts))
        return code

    fails: list[str] = []  # gh failures of the current poll

    def attempt(fn, *fargs):  # one gh-backed step: a failure is "unknown", retried on the next poll
        try:
            return fn(*fargs)
        except GhError as e:
            fails.append(str(e))
            errors.append(str(e))
            return None

    issue_comments = f"repos/{a.repo}/issues/{a.pr}/comments?per_page=100"

    def collect_findings() -> bool:
        if a.no_codex:
            return True
        review = attempt(fetch_review_state, a.repo, a.pr, head, a.since)
        if review is None:
            return False
        s["review"] = review
        if a.since:
            s["since"], s["since_source"] = a.since, "--since"
        return True

    while True:
        fails.clear()
        codex_polled = False
        s["ci_done"], s["codex_done"] = False, a.no_codex  # both re-judged on every poll, never latched
        cur = attempt(pr_head, a.repo, a.pr)
        if cur is not None and cur.lower() != head:
            return finish("head-moved", EXIT_MOVED, cur)
        if cur is not None:
            checks = attempt(fetch_checks, a.repo, head)
            if checks is not None:
                s["checks"] = checks
                s["ci_done"] = not any(c["state"] == "pending" for c in checks) and bool(
                    checks or time.monotonic() - start >= a.ci_grace
                )
            if not a.no_codex:
                comments = attempt(api_items, issue_comments)
                if comments is not None:
                    codex_polled = True
                    match, newest = codex_state_for(comments, head)
                    s["codex_match"], s["codex_newest"] = match, newest
                    if code_review(match).get("status") in TERMINAL:
                        s["codex_done"], s["running_seen"] = True, False
                    elif match is not None and not s["running_seen"]:
                        s["running_seen"] = True
                        s["codex_clock"] = time.monotonic()
            # finish only when both hold on this same poll
            if s["ci_done"] and s["codex_done"] and collect_findings():
                return finish("done", EXIT_DONE)

        now = time.monotonic()
        if codex_polled and not s["codex_done"] and now - s["codex_clock"] >= a.codex_stuck:
            return finish("codex-stuck", EXIT_STUCK)
        if now >= deadline:
            return finish("timeout", EXIT_GH if fails else EXIT_TIMEOUT)
        time.sleep(min(a.interval, max(0.0, deadline - now)))


def usage(msg: str) -> int:
    print(f"pr-wait: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    return EXIT_USAGE


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
